## What problem(s) does this PR solve?

- Takes over https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/120070
- Closes: https://github.com/godotengine/godot-proposals/issues/11819
- Mitigates: https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/78513

## Additional information

This PR mitigates the project assembly unload failure by ignoring it, keeping it leaked, and loading a new project assembly normally after it. The quote from the https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/120070 that is attributed to the [Unity staff member](https://discussions.unity.com/t/path-to-coreclr-2026-upgrade-guide/1714279/7) with the same idea: 
> > What happens when an assembly can’t be unloaded? What are the possible issues? Will there be two instances of types in that assembly: one from the newly loaded assembly and one from the one that couldn’t be unloaded? Will a newly instantiated object correctly use the new assembly?
> 
> Yes. We’re planning to report to the user after a suitable time that an AssemblyLoadContext has leaked.

PR:

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/99373a04-6d30-4b1d-8f36-5fa7261b36ae

### AI disclosure

The initial code was written by me, after the code was working I looked at the AI implementation from the https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/120070 to see if I missed anything... I missed `ReflectionUtils.cs`, so I also fixed it, and then I searched the Godot's codebase for other uses of `GetAssemblies` that could be affected and fixed it too (only found `CsTranslationParserPlugin.cs`). I didn't change the other code after seeing the AI implementation, tbh this part is correct:
> This PR is a Godot implementation of the same idea, and it's shocking how little code it takes to implement such.

So it is no wonder the implementations are still so similar.

Sidenote, it is kinda funny how confidently incorrect AI can be in the comments, the code is still correct for keeping the old behaviour, but the stated reasons are wrong.

<details>
<summary>AI code and wrong comments</summary>

```C#
    public static Type? FindTypeInLoadedAssemblies(string assemblyName, string typeFullName)
    {
        // With cooperative ALC unloading, there may be multiple assemblies with the same name
        // loaded from different ALCs (one from a leaked old ALC, one from the new ALC).
        // We must return the type from a non-leaked ALC. LastOrDefault prefers the most
        // recently loaded assembly (from the newest ALC), which is the correct one.
        // As an additional safety check, we filter out assemblies from ALCs that are being
        // unloaded (leaked), in case the enumeration order is not reliable.
        return AppDomain.CurrentDomain.GetAssemblies()
            .Where(a => a.GetName().Name == assemblyName)
            .Where(a =>
            {
                var alc = AssemblyLoadContext.GetLoadContext(a);
                // alc is null for assemblies in the default ALC (shared assemblies),
                // which are never being unloaded, so include them.
                return alc == null || !CustomGCHandle.IsAlcBeingUnloaded(alc);
            })
            .Select(a => a.GetType(typeFullName))
            .LastOrDefault(t => t != null);
    }
```
> LastOrDefault prefers the most recently loaded assembly (from the newest ALC), which is the correct one.

This may be true in implementation, but the order of returned from `GetAssemblies()` assemblies is not specified and the code shouldn't rely on it. `"in case the enumeration order is not reliable"` it is, so the whole chunk of the comment is just empty noise.

> alc is null for assemblies in the default ALC (shared assemblies), which are never being unloaded, so include them.

The Godot's `SharedAssemblies` do have their own `AssemblyLoadContext`, it seems to be how native hosting works.
<img width="1062" height="364" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8cc5e939-b6c5-4bda-81e7-1a1b58874df0" />
Not sure where it got `"assemblies in the default ALC"` returns null, I was convinced for a second at this part, but also checked the claim, and the shared assemblies from the default ALC correctly returns default ALC (I only now realised how obvious it sounds).
```C#
        var dalc = AssemblyLoadContext.GetLoadContext(typeof(object).Assembly);
        GD.Print($"Default ALC | {dalc}"); // Default ALC | "Default" System.Runtime.Loader.DefaultAssemblyLoadContext #3
```

Well, anyway it does need to pass the assemblies without ALC to keep the old behaviour, even if it seems you need really exotic assemblies to get it to return null context.

I don't get how people work with AI, making sure that everything it said is correct was exhausting, and after that I also needed to check the code too, I guess the code was alright, but after the comments that were either overly verbose and obvious or overly verbose and wrong, I also stopped trusting the code.

---
</details>

After such clearly wrong claims it is surprising that the used `CustomGCHandle.IsAlcBeingUnloaded` does work for tracking leaked assemblies. Test code:
```C#
    public void PrintALC()
    {
        HashSet<AssemblyLoadContext> set = [];
        foreach (var a in AppDomain.CurrentDomain.GetAssemblies())
        {
            var alc = AssemblyLoadContext.GetLoadContext(a);
            if (alc is not null)
            {
                if (set.Contains(alc))
                    continue;
                set.Add(alc);
                GD.Print($"{CustomGCHandle.IsAlcBeingUnloaded(alc)} | {alc} | {a.GetName()}");
            }
            else
            {
                GD.Print($"Null ALC | {a.GetName()}");
            }
        }
        var dalc = AssemblyLoadContext.GetLoadContext(typeof(object).Assembly);
        GD.Print($"Default ALC | {dalc}");
    }
```
Here I leaked 2 assemblies and it does keep them there, so it can be used for filtering:
<img width="1128" height="156" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f32760b8-f7cb-48d5-af8e-e5fb848ec57c" />

I also used similar print debugging inside the `ReflectionUtils.cs` and the `CsTranslationParserPlugin.cs` to be sure it correctly tracks the leaked assemblies, and it is also reported correctly here.

